5.5 Text File Data Storage

🎯 Key Concepts

Working with files always requires 3 essential steps:

Open the file (Establishes connection).

Process the file (Read or Write data).

Close the file (Disconnects program from file).

✍️ Writing Data (StreamWriter)

File.CreateText("filename.txt"): Creates a new file or overwrites an existing one.

File.AppendText("filename.txt"): Appends new text to an existing file without deleting existing contents.

Use @ for literal paths: File.AppendText(@"C:\Data\Names.txt").

StreamWriter outputFile = File.CreateText("courses.txt");
outputFile.WriteLine("Introduction to Computer Science"); // Writes text + newline
outputFile.Write("C# Programming");                       // Writes text without newline
outputFile.Close();                                        // ALWAYS close files!


📖 Reading Data (StreamReader)

File.OpenText("filename.txt"): Opens an existing file for reading.

inputFile.EndOfStream: Boolean property indicating if the end of the file is reached.

try
{
    StreamReader reader = File.OpenText("courses.txt");

    while (!reader.EndOfStream)
    {
        string line = reader.ReadLine();
        listBox1.Items.Add(line);
    }

    reader.Close();
}
catch (Exception ex)
{
    MessageBox.Show("Error reading file: " + ex.Message);
}


5.6 File Dialog Controls

🎯 Key Concepts

Invisible controls added to the Component Tray providing standard Windows file picker interfaces:

OpenFileDialog: Opens a file chooser dialog.

SaveFileDialog: Opens a "Save As" file chooser dialog.

ShowDialog(): Method that displays the dialog and returns a DialogResult (OK or Cancel).

💻 Code Example

// Reading a file selected via OpenFileDialog
if (openFileDialog1.ShowDialog() == DialogResult.OK)
{
    StreamReader reader = File.OpenText(openFileDialog1.FileName);
    listBox1.Items.Clear();

    while (!reader.EndOfStream)
    {
        listBox1.Items.Add(reader.ReadLine());
    }

    reader.Close();
}

// Writing to a file selected via SaveFileDialog
if (saveFileDialog1.ShowDialog() == DialogResult.OK)
{
    StreamWriter writer = File.CreateText(saveFileDialog1.FileName);
    writer.WriteLine("User output saved successfully.");
    writer.Close();
}


5.7 Random Numbers (Random Class)

🎯 Key Concepts

The Random class generates pseudo-random numbers.

Method

Description

Example

.Next()

Generates a random non-negative integer.

rand.Next()

.Next(max)

Generates a random integer from 0 to max - 1.

rand.Next(100) (0 to 99)

.Next(min, max)

Generates a random integer from min to max - 1.

rand.Next(1, 7) (1 to 6)

.NextDouble()

Generates a random double between 0.0 and 1.0.

rand.NextDouble()

💻 Code Example

Random rand = new Random();

// Generate a random dice roll (1 to 6)
int diceRoll = rand.Next(1, 7);

// Generate a percentage (0.0 to 1.0)
double percentage = rand.NextDouble();


5.8 The Form Load Event

🎯 Key Concepts

Triggered automatically when a form is loaded into memory before being displayed on screen.

Useful for initializing controls, loading saved settings, or filling ListBoxes.

💻 Code Example

private void Form1_Load(object sender, EventArgs e)
{
    // Executes automatically when application launches
    MessageBox.Show("Welcome to the Application!");
}
